  "source_name": "https://www.whattoexpect.com/pregnancy/week-by-week/week-35.aspx",
  "source_type": "website",
  "structure": {
    "block_count": 87,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "35 Weeks Pregnant",
        "text_sha256": "c4bcc6831f73cdc79c126c57f4a4d6a0dc59746e36ab22a7e9855df1787bacac",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby is as big as a bunch of Swiss chard.",
        "text_sha256": "dd9949bdd7edc8ed16c3ff260d8f616f651e37c5e3b90b58755b7ed4abeaae00",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "At the 35-week mark, you're wrapping up month 8 of your pregnancy with one month left to go.",
        "text_sha256": "6e8224d73c5b3307fe4a9f0e470759601c95167df1e97ede7842eebee22a283e",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your little one may be starting to make an exit plan by shifting to the heads-down position, all while working on important preparations such as building body fat and brain matter.",
        "text_sha256": "210984f34776924cf67eca7b539d8ec072ebc427b70e1261c1f0f7c3e54e9dec",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your body is also in prep mode: You may feel some Braxton Hicks contractions as your uterine muscles practice for the big day.",
        "text_sha256": "ce060a0ddd16c0cc6d44a5d8d265e61477f09b062bfb2ab11f2bc5cfd976e467",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Baby at Week 35",
        "text_sha256": "3f2be9c1846b5b02bfec0634a4d4ac04c39f389d4b21fd1dbf0417cfb3e6c27b",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "At a Glance",
        "text_sha256": "4007536f1ab858815b5f74377d1a44ff31bd2acc98e17bf65e9e39ed107102a1",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Snug as a bug",
        "text_sha256": "e4e7c9a39cd05cf00d1d38b298d3de54e7ba6fa8714eb4de4f7e16a43b00cc1d",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "With less space in your uterus now, your baby’s movements have shifted from kicks and punches to rolls and wiggles.",
        "text_sha256": "b256d03874f67c3a3f252edf06b8dcc4ed82b1830032994b387949d5ad3d38c1",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Padding up",
        "text_sha256": "d7a08a2eb8cf67411340c74db74c268ad0244aa8c4903fcbb227de67dcca77c6",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The majority of your baby’s growth now is fat. His shoulders in particular are padding up for the journey south.",
        "text_sha256": "90dc80b8d82a35a10378a4dfb922d6a82e72ce3317c75b5394ae8bd3b1c1afed",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Head down",
        "text_sha256": "02cdd385e2a63f3eb614a6aec3d47c432bb0fff6913a08a837f6cae196f2e61b",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "By this week, almost all babies are positioned with their heads facing down toward your cervix, ready for their trip out.",
        "text_sha256": "9976b41efc0967f19aa21e26b128b1b77e1d6eb49c7d3cab55bdd1237cf39314",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "35 weeks pregnant is how many months?",
        "text_sha256": "b666589a72ee48d802feb1598ad64c0a154e0201ffcc7232a7a2e84e6c0f6aa7",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you're 35 weeks pregnant, you're in month 8 of your pregnancy. Only 1 month left to go! Still have questions? Here's some more information on how weeks, months, and trimesters are broken down in pregnancy .",
        "text_sha256": "23fe2b5596190d0a5914298de5fb9cde5e0488a9ef6400ad305139ec900dff75",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "How big is my baby at 35 weeks?",
        "text_sha256": "889f71b6e7e743411be170f0f0e3c723a357b0cd7875ab5fd888b4c8460e6094",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby is standing tall this week at about 18 inches. But at about 5¼ pounds, he's continuing his steady weight gain: He'll pack on anywhere from 1 pound to several, including large amounts of baby fat, up 'til delivery day.",
        "text_sha256": "9c120760ca53da39a46c095d5075ae57c0f83afabca094848c3fe954ef544b97",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Fetal weight gain",
        "text_sha256": "f3a78b4fad41db0b3b4e95d4906790661da06ea59d40980298ccdd3a0b98c1e3",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby's once skinny arms and legs are now quite plump … and irresistibly, squeezably soft.",
        "text_sha256": "ec39064376b2331d586c71af64778e293e6db99fe596ccff7350f888b44ecbaf",
        "word_count": 16
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Baby's skull remains soft",
        "text_sha256": "4533133ccffd9e0b02804be59d279d662b336b2812b9525f2623ba5cc8629380",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Something else that's developing at a mind-boggling pace these day: your baby's brain power . Luckily, the part that surrounds that amazing brain — the skull — remains soft. And for good reason: A soft skull will allow your baby to squeeze more easily through the birth canal.",
        "text_sha256": "ca3754f8fc32e93ee53bf1658c10d2862a58988d0fcef41079475fd44649c5fb",
        "word_count": 48
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Body at Week 35",
        "text_sha256": "f5485d114b0928dc7e629b57e2d508079fc667d789828f11a04f81c5eb0b0bde",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Cheat sheet",
        "text_sha256": "9822b57849aa752652f8d911e1581f8709a4fd8204432c0ac12cc212908f1672",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Did you know that the measurement in centimeters from the top of your pubic bone to the top of your uterus is roughly equivalent to the number of weeks of pregnancy?",
        "text_sha256": "ebbc24d8175b6cbe579c0e6038866813f3a31988aaf72e74defe5d5be5d1502d",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "So when you're 35 weeks pregnant, your practitioner will likely be measuring about 35 centimeters on the tape measure. It's an easy way to remember how far along you are — as if you'd ever lose track.",
        "text_sha256": "8917d99efc04c0ee7924c522163d57fa850b6e3396856f8e392e9a984de3150d",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Frequent urination and urinary incontinence",
        "text_sha256": "0c7920a639fc0c3878631b25c3afc6c789452b09d2d94b7d05a3d6092375ed01",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Something else that'll remind you just how far along you are in your pregnancy: frequent urination . Yep, it's first trimester déjà vu all over again, but this time, instead of pregnancy hormones to blame, it's the position of your baby's head.",
        "text_sha256": "92c1034b1d404732ce852ad4a87e986220fc1425ca7dcdc30b11caa1c8c6fddd",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Now that your baby is head-down in preparation for delivery, his noggin is pressing squarely on your bladder. The result? A need to pee often.",
        "text_sha256": "407b27c1bc516a1f1f518879779d860bc28597df2e395294fab67e8b9b9febaf",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "As if that's not enough, you may also experience urgency — gotta go now ! — or a lack of bladder control when you cough, sneeze, or even laugh (though there's nothing funny about it).",
        "text_sha256": "cf6a38420f4ac9e8ee5a0e30f63411ae0362b2a2ed1aa3ff463715273036c48a",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Whatever you do, don't cut back on fluids. Instead, empty your bladder as completely as possible by leaning forward (just be careful not to tip over), wear a panty liner if you need one, and practice your Kegel exercises , which will strengthen the pelvic muscles and prevent or correct most cases of pregnancy-induced incontinence.",
        "text_sha256": "bf3cc5a57b3941755a2e83a5aae1f030ebe52a93f373b8727d78444a8e60a5be",
        "word_count": 55
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Finding a doula",
        "text_sha256": "e48464d718618fc25c036149da6c59977a6cd88076b993fad419a31726cd489f",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "What's a doula? The literal translation is \"woman's servant.\" Now, if only you could find a chef, massage therapist, and chauffeur …",
        "text_sha256": "01bd8417160ebbf09caf02f1ff19670d766e087dcf7e9d77c59ef97dbc8e6280",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A doula can do double duty when you need it the most. First, there's the birth doula, a companion who's professionally trained to provide support — and a hand to hold — during labor. The other is a postpartum one ( read more about them in week 42 ).",
        "text_sha256": "8fba1e7e7f3c582d793f5dc4affb6db4325f787ae543a868a75e9ca0eb8d0e5a",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A birth doula knows all about breathing and relaxation techniques for labor, as well as the best positions to alleviate the pain. Some laboring women actually find they need less pain medication — or none at all — when a doula is present, although a good birth doula will explore all pain relief options with you and help you choose what's right for your situation.",
        "text_sha256": "5776c7ba584d736d517669f314883a458be75e7334e013d6ff6e6db07352c259",
        "word_count": 65
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But a doula's most important role may be the emotional comfort provided during a potentially frightening time, and that might be just what the doctor — or midwife — ordered.",
        "text_sha256": "c689a593ee7efefc19fb98872f0c59e41b6e8522cde1b6fdaed4389a841f7c30",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "To find a doula, consult an agency, ask other moms for recommendations, and read more about hiring a doula .",
        "text_sha256": "4b7dcc4c4a0098a7069473324f449ebd8adee3cd2e0962df92d8abd356c47eab",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Pregnancy Symptoms Week 35",
        "text_sha256": "0e40fd19e27ac5ca84f48b4bd14880d79df45c9933de8bde8328af2eb3aaa987",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Occasional headaches",
        "text_sha256": "60898b860b179c2a381358b03b9cb009cdddf1bbe0baf9e4c153d439372956ba",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your head is pounding, it could be for a number of reasons, including being overheated or stuck in a stuffy room. Go out for some air or open the window. And ask your practitioner which pain reliever is safe in moderation during pregnancy. Most doctors will okay acetaminophen, as long as you don’t overdo it. If acetaminophen isn’t working, or if you’re getting a headache every day, call your doctor. Severe headaches that don’t respond to pain relievers like acetaminophen can be a sign of preeclampsia.",
        "text_sha256": "329d12c5414fa5c3f1ab03e3535d35b6001e8df355d25655b0e45fdb73acf068",
        "word_count": 87
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Pregnancy brain",
        "text_sha256": "2b86f7e34fd22a35cc48aab7dd82c2572550cd2007df723dd9b5c75d25dfad7c",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You’re probably getting more absentminded as you count down the weeks. Your brain cell volume really is shrinking and your bouts of sleeplessness don’t help, either. Expect the fog to lift a few months after your baby is born.",
        "text_sha256": "7bdbdb56e3fd1f3b86e6e9b46b98459839ff2ffd7af4a5c02058970295371322",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Braxton Hicks contractions",
        "text_sha256": "5285eeccdcc1c0a12b62bcdb1665797e938e1a2ac1ba0d3d5f0d33a4d9a51bf5",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You may be experiencing some contractions as your body gets ready for the real thing during labor. Haven’t felt one yet? First-time moms may not notice the flexing of their uterine muscles, which feels like your abdomen is tightening.",
        "text_sha256": "10d3e931b7fba4c0580e9733d2bc249f2fe0baae68b4f192ac736d126bd149b2",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Increasing clumsiness",
        "text_sha256": "d43a0f13fd7197e64d90a3d6dc937697bbd5ac059ca81133dafd51b8cbe813d3",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000044",
        "block_index": 44,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Balancing your body gets harder this week as you waddle toward the finish line — just a few more weeks to go! Continue to play it safe. If you need to reach for something on a high shelf, ask your partner instead of climbing on a chair to get it.",
        "text_sha256": "9bcdab632604f5b35d2bd2e90608e6c851413ecfc01cfeb3c1ef9f80086391ad",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000045",
        "block_index": 45,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Bleeding gums",
        "text_sha256": "3f189a5230f4bc8f637104d797dba7330408856782b0ab3582db45a5d86893eb",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000046",
        "block_index": 46,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your gums may still be bleeding or tender now. To boost gum strength, get plenty of vitamin C. Drink an extra glass of OJ, sprinkle berries on your oatmeal or cereal, and toss tomatoes into your salad.",
        "text_sha256": "a2a9ad4d304ff973a4dc657e26e557b78965d817588b44c93e1c96ad31da67ba",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000047",
        "block_index": 47,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Hemorrhoids",
        "text_sha256": "e738833d5a86a289cb9dc84d54eda9ef1ec35894b1337dc099fd151ec4f8441d",
        "word_count": 1
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000048",
        "block_index": 48,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Varicose veins can pop up anywhere — and the ones in your rectum are called hemorrhoids. To soothe them, gently wash with warm water and wipe with toilet paper. If toilet paper is too harsh, switch to wipes.",
        "text_sha256": "e0a3ebe158c7fdf11d67461b784f404df29565566fae58ef6fbff23aaf450860",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000049",
        "block_index": 49,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Skin rashes",
        "text_sha256": "1473ec2783457faf77c31e12d6e42f2feb4b7fe4cfefa07db3fbc14cfe721c22",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000050",
        "block_index": 50,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you’ve suddenly got an itchy, bumpy rash on your stomach, you could have PUPPP, which stands for “pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy.” The rashes are benign and cause no risk to you or your baby, but they’re annoying. To soothe the itch, try applying aloe vera gel after a shower or bath.",
        "text_sha256": "d708ab2a44cfd7dfcaabcc46c53bb4834e718b183d64d95492ffafe42d322669",
        "word_count": 55
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000051",
        "block_index": 51,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Varicose veins",
        "text_sha256": "ffa2cbf469d247a7e4946eba7b2ee403ddd5c3a14f91357bfafbde5235a52d1f",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000052",
        "block_index": 52,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Have the varicose veins in your legs begun to ache or itch? Though support hose probably don’t help cultivate that hot mama look you were going for, they can give the veins in your legs a little extra upward push to counteract the downward pull from your belly.",
        "text_sha256": "6a504b03456692e17e85d82d5666f557fcaa9ce2b9f0dd8f01f4fc02f3bffdc4",
        "word_count": 48
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000053",
        "block_index": 53,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Tips for You This Week",
        "text_sha256": "32770aa6fb9708009741b1a7b10691529ec1e23f79116914a353fdf3f2baf4e7",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000054",
        "block_index": 54,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Consider your child care options",
        "text_sha256": "92636ba9bb334cd7fc777f81302bce10c9d67232943d4e7c77b4c051c4a2b058",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000055",
        "block_index": 55,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Whether you’re working full-time or part-time, or simply need a few hours to yourself, it’s never too soon to start thinking about child care (especially because some day cares can have a year-long waitlist — or more!).",
        "text_sha256": "94fff123e0eafd6d0968a4edba74ea8cde4192b1834aeda840b239e6892ee121",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000056",
        "block_index": 56,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "(especially because some day cares can have a year-long waitlist — or more!).",
        "text_sha256": "630eb4041e6f40a996048e8f40e4e7653718868bf4794a782bbae257577112e4",
        "word_count": 13
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000057",
        "block_index": 57,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Some common options include day care centers and family day cares, which are run out of private homes, but there are also nannies (including overnight nannies), au pairs, relative caregivers, and babysitters.",
        "text_sha256": "df5747fd9757c1755e5e0871abd996fe4c30b37d23da8a80e4065033716176cd",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000058",
        "block_index": 58,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Start your search by considering what you can afford to pay each month, how many hours of care you’ll need, and how flexible or rigid your schedule is.",
        "text_sha256": "6d925a0ac64e435aad53843a10a1b978b8bce2892d9943b39702e30e0149873a",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000059",
        "block_index": 59,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Combat nasal congestion",
        "text_sha256": "57329700900b7acb13df6229434e1b7bd66f10c117c683a32c20131c4eae3544",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000060",
        "block_index": 60,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The pregnancy hormones estrogen and progesterone can make the mucus membranes in your nose swell, making you feel all stuffy. (Little wonder this symptom is known as pregnancy rhinitis.)",
        "text_sha256": "40c1bf994039775bc25112f2e19e0b1a896e6b0ae5236b5e9c24f866db20daa4",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000061",
        "block_index": 61,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "To find relief, buy a box of nasal strips that open up your nostrils or ask your doctor to recommend a saline spray that can help reduce mucus.",
        "text_sha256": "e07dc65793634ef781b3c72a76942058df8981ec96ccb9ee92079d7395c0ca67",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000062",
        "block_index": 62,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your nose is extra dry, dab a little petroleum jelly in each nostril and place a warm-mist humidifier in your room to add moisture to the air.",
        "text_sha256": "5df81ebb61dc32ff12adcb6efdeb013520183e1481d5497ab7133c2c157257b2",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000063",
        "block_index": 63,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Don't stress over stretch marks",
        "text_sha256": "84532d1faa108e330e8eccfb088f1beff610651f946b7527c306620a27d4c39c",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000064",
        "block_index": 64,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Try not to obsess about stretch marks . It's estimated that up to 90% of women get them, according to the American Academy of Dermatology (AAD), so you’re in fantastic company!",
        "text_sha256": "899368089da097469afbd61518737bbbc903685632656428485e94421a8ca4ef",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000065",
        "block_index": 65,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": ". It's estimated that up to 90% of women get them, according to the American Academy of Dermatology (AAD), so you’re in fantastic company!",
        "text_sha256": "55f3816380657ea9aec7b0902b16beff2e060380cd53a4bb6f8077473a28915f",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000066",
        "block_index": 66,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Over time, stretch marks gradually fade away on their own. In the meantime, you can try some lotions and other products to minimize them . And look on the bright side: They’re a sign your baby is growing beautifully.",
        "text_sha256": "102b263731bc2ee9e980778ff3dcf7896188415b93bb01cb0053c3a816503ce8",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000067",
        "block_index": 67,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Try to stave off pregnancy insomnia",
        "text_sha256": "827e79de1e8888378464e9bbde8cbba706671af030dcd45b65e6e6b98a1e7220",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000068",
        "block_index": 68,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Pregnancy insomnia affects an estimated 75% of moms-to-be — and it can hit especially hard in the third trimester (just when you need sleep the most!).",
        "text_sha256": "5ed6e25944fb88b57563ee2d876482a63e25d939800c757049387f7b1e6b1b0d",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000069",
        "block_index": 69,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "affects an estimated 75% of moms-to-be — and it can hit especially hard in the third trimester (just when you need sleep the most!).",
        "text_sha256": "8446a1b2755cd6eee2ba976b2ae756f6f5310a357f1c2c456617ef72b972d9a7",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000070",
        "block_index": 70,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "What’s more, even if you are tired, your burgeoning belly — and active baby — can prevent you from getting comfortable again and falling back to sleep.",
        "text_sha256": "663ea7675bac94fc2f4ff8e03ce8c34ad89d65448048d7265cd2d09325e25088",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000071",
        "block_index": 71,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Before you go to bed, try to wind down with a soothing bedtime routine: Do some light reading, take a warm bath, and try meditation or relaxation exercises.",
        "text_sha256": "8329907cf72965683d80fa6eab7990dc425e2f940b3aeacfec6c3fe3b852e7d4",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000072",
        "block_index": 72,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you can’t drift off after 20 to 30 minutes, get out of bed and find a small, boring task to do — like sorting baby onesies. Then try to nod off again.",
        "text_sha256": "99a1a91a862ec977309927c136f24aa24ddcaa4017c5c9af41366a422dae797a",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000073",
        "block_index": 73,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Relieve heartburn",
        "text_sha256": "1b87e9782a592d905f658cf8b36942f7b2be41f9b6e606fb6368b26f139717b0",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000074",
        "block_index": 74,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Nearly half of all moms-to-be have heartburn during pregnancy — so if you’re feeling the burn, rest assured you’re in good company.",
        "text_sha256": "0ae563c5f358a209e4b07fc62756b99a08efd746d13a173552f7dac1e18d7190",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000075",
        "block_index": 75,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "— so if you’re feeling the burn, rest assured you’re in good company.",
        "text_sha256": "b45b8d19a06bee933a02fc7b8122d9a4770fae44b648a3d2a60c422ba82baec2",
        "word_count": 13
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000076",
        "block_index": 76,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Rule number one: Make sure to sit upright while eating and stay that way for a couple of hours after you eat if possible.",
        "text_sha256": "5d45659b06897009c6479ea681725682409c783bbe0dce9c0711f4ebde99a256",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000077",
        "block_index": 77,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Lying down, slouching, slumping, and stooping will do you in. When you have to bend, do it with your knees instead of your waist — otherwise, you'll send all that acid for a repeat ride up your esophagus.",
        "text_sha256": "50868ef36e3851bde3f617c179e0bdbf5f695aa2821bc8b0cab3c044c13713ef",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000078",
        "block_index": 78,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "While you're eating, try to avoid triggers like citrus and tomato (unfortunately that includes your pizza sauce!). And do your body a favor: Eat slowly. Chewing is the first step in the digestive process, but it's the one that's most often neglected when eating's done on the run. The more you chew, the less work your stomach has to do.",
        "text_sha256": "837c7c5b2c7d71119dcd8a3d65c4b008820073b4350acbfe7c7e94275f3e66d3",
        "word_count": 60
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000079",
        "block_index": 79,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Another chew that's good for you: sugarless gum, which will increase saliva and help neutralize acid in the esophagus. Just don't overdo it — too much can cause a laxative effect thanks to the sugar alcohols in it.",
        "text_sha256": "785298168255af9cc8361b4d32795e0529465751f7c7a7b7e8915671b5bda4a0",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000080",
        "block_index": 80,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Lastly, try to leave stress off the menu whenever possible (easier said than done, we know) — anxious eating compounds digestive distresses of all kinds, including heartburn.",
        "text_sha256": "9df90ddb6240a55fcbbe2dd489fb0b5746114c448e0c066f45865c9a0f9f0b7e",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000081",
        "block_index": 81,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Prep for your hospital stay",
        "text_sha256": "8ea6263e2b0820714dca2823a091e89af8895ddf04a5a3d4320d2eb6bc5d4c75",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000082",
        "block_index": 82,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It never hurts to be prepared, especially before you go into labor. To get ready for your hospital stay , scout out the entrance — some hospitals require you to enter through the emergency room — and figure out where you’ll park the car.",
        "text_sha256": "20d06b28cf8e1a3fba8238a8d87a0d865659e9a140ef2e916208f2a5c5be60ae",
        "word_count": 44
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000083",
        "block_index": 83,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you haven’t driven to the hospital yet, map out the most direct route and consider doing a dry run with your partner or labor coach. If you have children at home, line up babysitting patrol and ask a family member or friend to feed your pets, if you have them.",
        "text_sha256": "4377ad80b97510387fe038e6383f8c78b3fe7968a3236f58748b763859010512",
        "word_count": 51
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000084",
        "block_index": 84,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Do squat exercises",
        "text_sha256": "b1253b3fc841d580b9a3a9109b056146d537d840b94fcaf3eb5ea716aadbddc0",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000085",
        "block_index": 85,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Ever heard about women of yore squatting wherever they happened to be to give birth? Well, it turns out there's something to it: Squatting actually speeds labor because it increases the pelvic opening, providing more room for baby to descend.",
        "text_sha256": "95c85096c7d5cbfe531e93f08d2a7b2e7be11a1c509bbe150290419c601dea9d",
        "word_count": 40
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000086",
        "block_index": 86,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Don't worry — this isn't to suggest that you take to the fields when those contractions start coming. But start working squats into your workout routine now so you'll be a stronger squatter when the time comes — in the hospital or birthing center, that is.",
        "text_sha256": "469f7820c900456f60fe67f891c6bbbf26261888beb1abb59abe899514ef3702",
        "word_count": 46
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 2030,
